.\" sball-addition manual page
.\" Format with: groff -man -Tutf8 docs/sball-addition.8 | less
.TH SBALL-ADDITION 8 "2026-10-14" "sball-addition 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sball-addition \- managed hook system for the sballd (PVM) service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sball-addition
[\fIglobal options\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIcommand options\fR]
.br
.B sballd-wrapper
[\fIsballd arguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sball-addition
runs administrator supplied scripts (hooks) at two points of the
.BR sballd (8)
lifecycle:
.TP
.B pre-quorate
before
.B sballd
starts, while the service is still down. A failing hook can abort the start.
.TP
.B post-quorate
after the node has joined the PVM cluster and reports the
.I quorate
state. The node is fully operational at this point, which makes the phase
suitable for registering with monitoring, synchronising configuration or
warming caches.
.PP
The pre-quorate phase is driven by
.BR sballd-wrapper ,
which replaces
.B sballd
as the entry point of the service unit and
.BR exec s
the real daemon once the hooks succeeded. The post-quorate phase is driven by
.BR "sball-addition watcher" ,
a small process that polls the PVM XML-RPC socket (default
.IR 127.0.0.1:7090 )
until the local node reports
.IR quorate .
.PP
Every action is written to a JSON lines log, so
.BR jq (1)
can be used for queries and alerting.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI list " [\-\-phase=" phase "] [\-\-json] [\-\-strict]"
Show every hook in execution order with phase, order, name, enabled flag,
target and description. Execution order is: numeric filename prefix (so
.I 10\-foo.sh
runs before
.IR 20\-bar.sh ),
then the
.B AFTER
dependency, with ties broken by filename.
.TP
.BI run " hook-name [\-\-phase=" phase "] [\-\-force] [\-\-no\-state] [\-\-quiet] [\-\-dry\-run]"
Run a single hook immediately, honouring TIMEOUT, RETRY, RETRY_BACKOFF, TARGET
and RUN_ONCE.
.B \-\-dry\-run
reports what would happen (including every skip decision) without executing the
hook.
.B \-\-force
ignores a RUN_ONCE marker;
.B \-\-no\-state
leaves state and runtime files untouched.
The hook's stdout/stderr are shown unless
.B \-\-quiet
is given. The exit status is the hook's exit status.
.TP
.BI run\-phase " phase [\-\-force] [\-\-quiet] [\-\-json] [\-\-dry\-run]"
Run every hook of a phase in order. This is the command
.B sballd-wrapper
uses; it exits non-zero when a hook with
.B ON_FAIL=abort
failed.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the resolved execution order with timeouts, retry counts, targets and
.IR ON_FAIL
policies, performs all skip decisions and executes nothing - the safe way to
preview a service start.
.B \-\-json
turns the same information into a machine readable summary.
.TP
.B status [\-\-json]
Show the version, sballd pid/state/uptime, the cluster master, watcher state,
hook counts and the most recent pre/post-quorate hook outcome.
.TP
.BI log " [\-\-follow] [\-\-hook=" name "] [\-\-phase=" phase "] [\-\-since=" time "] [\-\-level=" level "] [\-\-limit=" n "] [\-\-pretty] [\-\-file=" path "]"
Print the JSON log. Raw records are printed by default so the output can be
piped into
.BR jq ;
.B \-\-pretty
renders a compact human readable form.
.B \-\-since
accepts a duration
.RI ( 10m ", " 2h ", " 1d )
or a timestamp.
.TP
.BI enable " hook-name [\-\-phase=" phase "]"
.TP
.BI disable " hook-name [\-\-phase=" phase "]"
Flip the
.B ENABLED
field inside the hook header. The hook file stays the single source of truth,
so the change survives configuration management and is visible in
.BR list .
.TP
.BI install\-hook " file \-\-phase=" phase " [\-\-order=" n "] [\-\-force]"
Validate a hook script and install it into the phase directory, mode 0755. The
header must declare the same phase as
.BR \-\-phase .
.TP
.BI uninstall\-hook " hook-name [\-\-phase=" phase "] [\-\-keep\-state]"
Remove a hook script together with its RUN_ONCE marker.
.TP
.B validate [\-\-phase=\fIphase\fR] [\-\-json]
Check the configuration, directories, permissions, hook headers, ordering and
socket reachability. Exit status 4 when a check fails.
.TP
.B watcher [\-\-foreground] [\-\-once] [\-\-poll\-interval=\fIseconds\fR] [\-\-poll\-timeout=\fIseconds\fR] [\-\-force]
Wait for the quorate state, then run the post-quorate hooks.
Without
.B \-\-foreground
the watcher daemonises itself (fork/setsid) and the command returns
immediately; systemd and
.B sballd-wrapper
use
.BR \-\-foreground .
.B \-\-once
polls a single time and exits without an error when the node is not quorate
yet, which is convenient for cron and for tests.
Exit status 5 means another watcher already holds the lock, exit status 6 means
the poll timeout expired.
.TP
.B metrics [\-\-out=\fIfile\fR] [\-\-no\-socket] [\-\-json]
Print the node and hook state in the Prometheus text exposition format (alias
.BR export\-metrics ).
Without
.B \-\-out
the exposition goes to stdout;
.B \-\-out
writes it atomically (temporary file plus rename, the directory is created when
needed), which is what a node_exporter textfile collector wants.
.B \-\-no\-socket
skips the PVM query; the
.IR pvm_socket_reachable ,
.I quorate
and
.I is_master
series are then omitted instead of being reported as 0, so an alert on
.B sball_addition_quorate == 0
does not fire on a node that was simply never asked.
.B \-\-json
prints the same snapshot as JSON.
See the
.I monitoring
section of PRODUCTION.md for the alert rules.
.TP
.B version [\-\-json]
Print the version, commit and build date.
.TP
.B help
Show the command overview.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root= dir
Installation root used to resolve
.IR /etc/sball-addition " and " /var/lib/sball-addition .
Defaults to the
.B SBALL_ADDITION_ROOT
environment variable or
.IR / .
.TP
.BI \-\-config= file
Configuration file, default
.IR <root>/etc/sball-addition/sball-addition.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level= level
Overrides
.BR general.log_level
(debug, info, warn, error).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file= file
Overrides the log destination.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket= host:port
Overrides the PVM XML-RPC socket.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-log
Do not write to the log file (useful for read only invocations).
.SH HOOKS
A hook is an executable script whose header describes it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
#!/bin/bash
### BEGIN SBALL-ADDITION INFO
# HOOK: post-quorate
# NAME: register-monitoring
# DESCRIPTION: Register node in monitoring system
# TIMEOUT: 60
# RETRY: 2
# RETRY_BACKOFF: 5
# ON_FAIL: continue
# RUN_ONCE: false
# AFTER: network-check
# TARGET: local
# ENABLED: true
### END SBALL-ADDITION INFO
.RE
.fi
.PP
Fields:
.TP
.B HOOK
required,
.I pre\-quorate
or
.IR post\-quorate .
.TP
.B NAME
required, unique inside the phase, used by
.BR run ,
.BR enable " and " uninstall\-hook .
.TP
.B TIMEOUT
seconds before the hook is SIGTERMed (SIGKILL after 5 more seconds). Defaults
to
.BR general.default_timeout .
.TP
.B RETRY / RETRY_BACKOFF
extra attempts and the delay between them.
.TP
.B ON_FAIL
.IR abort
stops the phase (and, for pre-quorate, the service start) when the hook fails
after all retries;
.I continue
logs the failure and carries on.
.TP
.B RUN_ONCE
when
.IR true ,
a marker is written to
.I /var/lib/sball-addition/state/<phase>/<name>.state
and the hook is skipped on later runs unless
.B \-\-force
is used.
.TP
.B AFTER
name of a hook in the same phase that must run first. Missing names and cycles
are configuration errors.
.TP
.B TARGET
.IR local
(this node),
.I master
(only when this node is the cluster master) or
.I all
(every node; in the current MVP every node simply runs its own copy).
.TP
.B ENABLED
.IR true " or " false .
.PP
The runner exports the following variables to every hook: SBALL_HOOK_PHASE,
SBALL_HOOK_NAME, SBALL_HOSTNAME, SBALL_MASTER_ID, SBALL_IS_MASTER,
SBALL_QUORATE_STATE, SBALL_LOG_FILE, SBALL_HOOK_PATH, SBALL_HOOK_DIR,
SBALL_STATE_DIR, SBALL_STATE_FILE, SBALL_RUNTIME_DIR, SBALL_COMMON_LIB,
SBALL_RUN_ID, SBALL_RETRY_ATTEMPT, SBALL_RETRY_MAX and
SBALL_ADDITION_VERSION.
.PP
Sourcing
.I /etc/sball-addition/lib/common.sh
provides
.BR sball_info ", " sball_warn ", " sball_error ", " sball_retry ", " sball_run ,
.BR sball_require " and friends."
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/sball-addition/sball-addition.conf
.PP
.nf
.RS
[general]
log_level = info
log_file = /var/log/sball-addition/sball-addition.log
socket_host = 127.0.0.1
socket_port = 7090
default_timeout = 60

[pre-quorate]
enabled = true
on_failure = abort
continue_on_empty = true

[post-quorate]
enabled = true
poll_interval_seconds = 2
poll_timeout_seconds = 300
on_failure = continue
.RE
.fi
.PP
The file is optional; the values above are the built-in defaults.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
a hook failed or a phase was aborted
.TP
.B 2
usage error
.TP
.B 3
configuration missing or invalid
.TP
.B 4
hook not found, hook set invalid, validation failure
.TP
.B 5
another watcher already runs on this node (lock held)
.TP
.B 6
the quorate poll timeout expired
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sball-addition/sball-addition.conf
Configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/sball-addition/hooks.d/pre-quorate.d/
Pre-quorate hooks.
.TP
.I /etc/sball-addition/hooks.d/post-quorate.d/
Post-quorate hooks.
.TP
.I /etc/sball-addition/lib/common.sh
Helper library for hooks.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sball-addition/state/<phase>/<name>.state
RUN_ONCE markers.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sball-addition/runtime/{watcher.pid,watcher.lock,last-run.json}
Watcher pid file, watcher lock and the last outcome per hook.
.TP
.I /var/log/sball-addition/sball-addition.log
JSON log (rotated with
.BR logrotate (8),
the watcher reopens it on SIGHUP).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SBALL_ADDITION_ROOT
Alternative installation root.
.TP
.B SBALL_ADDITION_{LOG_LEVEL,LOG_FILE,SOCKET_HOST,SOCKET_PORT}
Overrides for the configuration file.
.TP
.B SBALLD_BIN
Real daemon used by the wrapper (default
.I /usr/sbin/sballd.real
when present, else
.IR /usr/sbin/sballd ).
.TP
.B SBALL_ADDITION_BIN
CLI used by the wrapper (default
.IR /usr/bin/sball-addition ).
.TP
.B SBALL_ADDITION_WRAPPER_ARGS
Extra CLI arguments for the wrapper, e.g.
.IR "\-\-log\-level=debug" .
.TP
.B SBALL_ADDITION_REQUIRED
When set to 1 the wrapper refuses to start sballd if the CLI is missing
(default: fail open).
.TP
.B SBALL_ADDITION_SKIP_WRAPPER
When set to 1 the wrapper starts sballd without running any hook (emergency
switch).
.SH PRODUCTION
Two helper commands are installed next to
.BR sball-addition :
.TP
.B sball-node-verify [\-\-smoke] [\-\-json] [\-\-root=\fIdir\fR]
Acceptance check for one node: binary, configuration, hook headers and ordering,
hook file permissions, wrapper, systemd wiring, watcher process, state/runtime/log
directories, PVM socket and the resolved pre-quorate plan. With
.B \-\-smoke
it installs, runs and removes a temporary hook. Exit status 1 means "do not
restart this node yet".
.TP
.B sball-rollout \-\-nodes=\fIfile\fR [\-\-rpm=\fIfile\fR|\-\-tarball=\fIfile\fR]
Cluster rollout over ssh: copies and installs the artifact, runs
.BR "sball-addition validate" ,
restarts
.BR sballd.service ,
waits for the node to become quorate again
.RB ( \-\-wait\-quorate ,
default 300 s) and runs
.BR sball-node-verify .
Non-master nodes are rolled out first, master nodes last
.RB ( \-\-no\-master\-last
keeps the given order), the rollout stops on the first failure unless
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
is given.
.B \-\-dry\-run
and
.B \-\-list
show the plan without executing anything.
.PP
See
.I PRODUCTION.md
in the distribution for the complete deployment, monitoring and rollback
runbook.

.SH EXAMPLES
Show the installed hooks:
.PP
.RS
sball-addition list
.RE
.PP
Run one hook now and ignore its RUN_ONCE marker:
.PP
.RS
sball-addition run register-monitoring \-\-force
.RE
.PP
Preview what a service start would execute:
.PP
.RS
sball-addition run-phase pre-quorate \-\-dry\-run
.RE
.PP
Check a node before restarting it:
.PP
.RS
sball-node-verify \-\-smoke
.RE
.PP
Watch the log of the post-quorate phase:
.PP
.RS
sball-addition log \-\-phase=post-quorate \-\-follow \-\-pretty
.RE
.PP
Find every failed hook execution of the last hour:
.PP
.RS
sball-addition log \-\-since=1h | jq -c 'select(.event=="fail")'
.RE
.PP
Wait for quorate in a script without starting a service:
.PP
.RS
sball-addition watcher \-\-once || echo "not quorate yet"
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1),
.BR logrotate (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
The project README documents the wrapper, the RPM and the integration modes;
.I PRODUCTION.md
covers building the release bundle, the quorum-safe cluster rollout, monitoring,
upgrades and rollback.
